<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victoria and Albert Museum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as V&amp;A</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Victoria and Albert Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> is the world's largest museum of decorative arts and design, founded in 1852 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> and given its present name in 1899 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and Prince Albert. Its collections span ceramics, textiles, furniture, sculpture, fashion and Asian art. Among its best-known objects is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipus-tiger">Tipu's Tiger</a>, a mechanical organ taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>'s palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a> in 1799.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victoria-and-albert-museum.jpg" alt="Victoria and Albert Museum" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alan Fletcher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victoria_and_Albert_Museum_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
